We obviously have $\mathrm{a)}\Rightarrow\mathrm{b)}\Rightarrow\mathrm{c)}\unsure{=|\Rightarrow}\mathrm{d)}$, and $d\Rightarrow a$ should be more or less known, or at least be provable with the help of well-known things. These conditions imply e, but I have no idea at the moment how to see that e) implies good reduction --- would need an understanding (\unsure{be it} heuristic) more thorough than the one I have at the moment.
\margin{It would be worth looking at a \unsure{version} of this where $X_\eta=\hat U_\eta$ has good reduction --- but the multisection $\overline{X_\eta-U_\eta}$ of $X$ over $S$ is not étale. The genus $0$ case should be particularly easy to look at\ldots}

